    "description": "Visits among relatives, friends, and neighbors are an important setting for Iranian hospitality and reciprocal social ties. Historical accounts and contemporary etiquette guidance describe recognizable conventions, while leaving considerable room for household preference.",
    "tags": ["Visiting", "Hospitality", "Family", "Nowruz", "Social customs"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:43:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:43:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Visiting Customs in Iran: Homes and Reciprocal Visits | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iranian household visits, reciprocal hospitality, Nowruz gatherings, and the importance of interpreting etiquette in its household context."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reciprocal-visits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Visiting as an ongoing relationship",
        "content": "The Persian expression did o bazdid refers to exchanging visits: calling on someone and receiving a return visit belong to a continuing relationship. Encyclopaedia Iranica places these encounters alongside congratulations, condolences, and family celebrations. Its historical account draws on travel writing to describe established conventions of greeting, reception, and departure. [s1]\n\nThat historical depth should not be mistaken for an unchanged national script. The same account notes abbreviated ceremonies among younger and more modernized social circles. Its value is in showing how visits have expressed recognition and reciprocal attention, rather than proving that every detail remains customary in every home. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "arrival-and-seating",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The household provides the immediate cues",
        "content": "Cultural Atlas advises visitors to arrive punctually, follow the host's example concerning shoes, and acknowledge elders first. It also describes floor seating in some traditional households and advises against stretching one's legs toward other guests. These are orientation points rather than a guarantee of what a particular home will look like. [s2]\n\nIts account describes separate socializing by men and women as associated with particularly conservative households. A visitor should therefore avoid assuming either separation or mixed company in advance. The editorial implication is simple: let the actual host explain the arrangements, and treat descriptions of customary behavior as context rather than instructions to impose on the family. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "refreshments-and-conversation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Receiving attention",
        "content": "Tea, fruit, sweets, and other refreshments appear prominently in Iranica's historical account of social calls. Offering them verbally, rather than merely placing them within reach, forms part of the host's attention to the guest. The article also records changing conventions, including the decline of older serving sequences as invitations acquired specified times. [s1]\n\nThese details help explain why a visit cannot be understood solely as a meal or an appointment. Reception itself carries meaning. Nevertheless, an account of past hospitality cannot establish a required menu, a compulsory number of servings, or the correct duration of a visit today. Those questions remain matters for the particular household.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "nowruz-visits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Nowruz and the renewal of ties",
        "content": "Nowruz, the spring New Year celebration shared across Iran and neighboring societies, gives visiting a particularly visible seasonal setting. UNESCO's description identifies visits to relatives, especially older people, and neighbors among its practices. Shared meals and gifts also form part of that account. [s3]\n\nThe description concerns a multinational heritage tradition, so it cannot establish an identical itinerary for every Iranian family. It does show why visiting belongs within a wider celebration of renewal: relationships are enacted through spending time together. UNESCO also identifies observation and participation as ways these practices pass between generations. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "leaving-well",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A considerate departure",
        "content": "Cultural Atlas notes that farewells can take time and that guests may need to reiterate their departure courteously. [s2] The broader lesson is to recognize the attention involved in hosting without turning etiquette into a performance. Household cues and clear communication provide a better basis for a comfortable visit than an imagined set of universally binding rules.",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
