    "description": "Gift giving in Iran can express appreciation, commemorate an occasion, or sustain a relationship across visits and journeys. Its domestic forms should be distinguished from the tribute and patronage exchanges documented in historical courts.",
    "tags": ["Gifts", "Reciprocity", "Hospitality", "Nowruz", "Social history"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:43:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:43:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Gift Giving in Iran: Occasions and Reciprocity | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Iranian gift-giving customs, modest household presents, travel gifts, Nowruz exchanges, and the limits of historical comparisons."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "everyday-gifts",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A gesture suited to the occasion",
        "content": "A gift can acknowledge hospitality without being elaborate. Cultural Atlas identifies sweets, flowers, and pastries as small presents associated with visiting an Iranian home. Its guidance also emphasizes careful presentation, modest language from the giver, and appreciative reception. These conventions frame giving as an exchange of consideration rather than a display of purchasing power. [s1]\n\nThe guide describes receiving a gift with both hands and leaving it unopened in the giver's presence. Such observations are useful preparation, but they should not become tests of another person's cultural authenticity. The source is a general orientation guide; it does not demonstrate that every family follows the same sequence. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "journeys-and-memory",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Gifts brought home",
        "content": "Persian gift vocabulary preserves distinctions among occasions and relationships. In Willem Floor's account of the Qajar period, sowghat, armaghan, and rahavard denote presents brought back from a journey for friends at home. Yadgar refers to a memento associated with an absent friend. The article also explains that some terms carried different meanings in earlier periods. [s2]\n\nThis vocabulary helps distinguish the object from its social purpose. A travel present marks remembrance across separation; a memento sustains an association with its giver. These are historical meanings documented by the source, not evidence that every contemporary speaker uses the words in precisely the same way. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "seasonal-and-family-occasions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Celebrations and reciprocal attention",
        "content": "Floor describes gifts accompanying visits, marriages, and holidays, including ordinary presents of candy and fruit. His account also records New Year exchanges within extended families and distinguishes these from gifts involving rulers and their dependents. Gift giving therefore appears across different social settings, but its meaning changes with the relationship. [s2]\n\nUNESCO's account of Nowruz, the spring New Year celebration, likewise identifies exchanged gifts, especially for children, alongside visits and shared meals. Because this is a description of a multinational tradition, it supports the broad connection between seasonal celebration and giving without establishing a single Iranian gift list or spending expectation. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-power",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "When a historical gift was an obligation",
        "content": "Historical court exchanges require a different interpretation. Rudi Matthee's account of Safavid gift giving explains that pishkash, a presentation from a subordinate to a superior, could function within systems of tribute and patronage. Some exchanges were regulated obligations rather than voluntary personal surprises. Gifts helped express and reproduce differences in power. [s4]\n\nMatthee also warns that surviving information about premodern gift giving is heavily concentrated on political elites. That imbalance matters: an archive rich in diplomatic presents cannot, by itself, reveal what ordinary households regularly exchanged. Courtly magnificence should not become a model for interpreting a box of pastries brought to a friend's home. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-gesture",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Relationship before price",
        "content": "Read together, these sources suggest that occasion, relationship, and historical setting explain more than price alone. A household present, a travel keepsake, a child's New Year gift, and an obligatory court presentation belong to different kinds of exchange. Recognizing those distinctions makes the subject more intelligible without inventing a universal rule about how much someone should give or what must be returned. [s1][s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
